"## Brazilian Portuguese (pt-BR) number, currency, and percent formatting conventions: decimal comma, thousands separator, currency symbol placement, R$ usage, negative numbers\n# Brazilian Portuguese (pt-BR) Formatting Conventions\n\n## Numbers\n- **Decimal separator:** comma — `3,14`\n- **Thousands separator:** period — `1.234.567,89`\n- Grouping is in threes; no special grouping for 4-digit years, postal codes, etc.\n- Number words use the **short scale**: *mil* (10³), *milhão* (10⁶), *bilhão* (10⁹), *trilhão* (10¹²) [1][3]\n- CLDR decimal pattern: `#,##0.###` with `latn` (Western) digits [1]\n\n## Currency (BRL — Brazilian Real)\n- **Symbol:** `R$` (ISO 4217: BRL, numeric 986); subunit *centavos* (1/100) [4]\n- **Placement:** symbol **before** the amount, separated by a (non-breaking) space — `R$ 1.234,56` [1][4]\n- CLDR pattern: `¤ #,##0.00` → renders as `R$ 1.234,56` [1]\n- The space is standard in official usage (Banco Central do Brasil, major style guides); informal writing sometimes omits it, but implementations should keep it [4]\n- Typographic note: the Brazilian *cifrão* is traditionally drawn with two strokes, but in plain text/Unicode it is simply `R$` [1]\n\n## Negative Numbers\n- Standard: **minus sign prefix** — `-1.234,56`\n- Negative currency per CLDR: pattern `-¤ #,##0.00` → `-R$ 1.234,56` (minus before the symbol) [1]\n- Parenthesized negatives `(R$ 1.234,56)` appear only in some accounting spreadsheets/reports; they are **not** the locale standard [1][3]\n- Some software renders `R$ -1.234,56`; this is a variant, not CLDR-conformant [1]\n\n## Percent\n- Sign **follows** the number, **no space** — `50%`, `12,5%` [1][3]\n- CLDR patterns: `#,##0%` (positive), `-%,##0%`-style negative → `-50%` [1]\n- Financial rates often add qualifiers: `12,5% a.a.` (per year), `1,2% a.m.` (per month) [4]\n\n## Implementation Summary (CLDR/ICU pt-BR)\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Decimal | `,` |\n| Group | `.` |\n| Decimal pattern | `#,##0.###` |\n| Currency pattern | `¤ #,##0.00` |\n| Negative currency | `-¤ #,##0.00` |\n| Percent pattern | `#,##0%` |\n\n## Sources\n[1] Unicode CLDR, locale data for pt-BR (patterns, separators, currency/percent formats) — cldr.unicode.org\n[2] ICU (International Components for Unicode) locale documentation — unicode-org.github.io/icu\n[3] Microsoft Go-Global / Windows locale reference for Portuguese (Brazil)\n[4] Banco Central do Brasil — official currency usage and BRL specifications (bcb.gov.br)\n\n---\n\n## Brazilian Portuguese date and time formats: short/medium/long date patterns, 12h vs 24h clock, first day of week, month and weekday names, ISO 8601 usage\n# Brazilian Portuguese (pt-BR) Date & Time Conventions\n\n## Date patterns\n\n| Style | Pattern | Example (2026-09-25) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Short | dd/MM/yyyy | 25/09/2026 |\n| Medium | d 'de' MMM 'de' yyyy | 25 de set. de 2026 |\n| Long | d 'de' MMMM 'de' yyyy | 25 de setembro de 2026 |\n| Full | EEEE, d 'de' MMMM 'de' yyyy | sexta-feira, 25 de setembro de 2026 |\n\n- The day/month/year order with slashes is the universal everyday format; the \"de\" connectors are required in written-out dates [1][4].\n- Cardinal numbers are used for the day (dia 25, not \"vigésimo quinto\"); month and weekday names are lowercase in running text [1].\n\n## Time formats\n\n- CLDR standard patterns for pt-BR are 24-hour: short **HH:mm**, medium **HH:mm:ss**; full/long use the characteristic notation **HH'h'mm'mm'ss'ss'** (e.g., 14h30min25s) plus zone [1][4].\n- The \"h\" separator (14h30, 9h15min) is the dominant convention in Brazilian writing.\n- In everyday speech, the 12-hour clock with qualifiers is common (\"às 3 da tarde\", \"9 da manhã\"), but formal/technical formatting is 24-hour [1][4].\n\n## First day of week\n\n- **Sunday (domingo)** is the first day of the week in Brazil (CLDR week data: BR = sun) [2].\n- Consequently Monday is *segunda-feira* (\"second day\"), through Friday *sexta-feira*; Saturday and Sunday are *sábado* and *domingo*.\n\n## Month and weekday names\n\n- **Months:** janeiro, fevereiro, março, abril, maio, junho, julho, agosto, setembro, outubro, novembro, dezembro. Abbreviated: jan., fev., mar., abr., mai., jun., jul., ago., set., out., nov., dez. [1]\n- **Weekdays:** domingo, segunda-feira, terça-feira, quarta-feira, quinta-feira, sexta-feira, sábado. Abbreviated: dom., seg., ter., qua., qui., sex., sáb. [1]\n\n## ISO 8601 usage\n\n- Brazil has adopted ISO 8601 as a national standard via **ABNT NBR ISO 8601** (latest edition aligned with ISO 8601-1:2019) [3]. The YYYY-MM-DD format is standard in technical, scientific, IT, and data-exchange contexts.\n- ABNT bibliographic style (NBR 6023) writes dates as \"25 set. 2026\" in citations [3].\n- In daily life, dd/mm/yyyy prevails; ISO format is not used colloquially.\n\n## Related notes\n\n- **Time zone:** Brasília Time (BRT, UTC−3) is the de facto national reference; the country spans UTC−2 (Fernando de Noronha) to UTC−5. **Daylight saving time was abolished in 2019** by federal decree [5].\n- Decimal comma / thousands point apply to numeric formatting generally [4].\n\n**Sources:** [1] Unicode CLDR pt-BR locale data (date/time patterns) · [2] CLDR Supplemental Week Data (territory first-day) · [3] ABNT NBR ISO 8601 / NBR 6023 standards · [4] Microsoft Windows locale documentation (pt-BR) · [5] Decreto federal nº 9.772/2019 (extinção do horário de verão)\n\n---\n\n## CLDR plural rules for Portuguese (pt and pt-BR): one/many/other categories, examples with numbers, ordinal plural rules, ICU MessageFormat syntax for pt-BR\n# CLDR Plural Rules for Portuguese (pt / pt-PT / pt-BR)\n\n**Key fact:** CLDR has no separate plural data for pt-BR — it inherits from root `pt`. `pt`, `pt-PT`, and `pt-BR` share **identical cardinal rules**. Categories: **one, many, other** (`many` was added in CLDR 42 / ICU 72, Oct 2022, for compact/million-scale numbers) [1][3][7].\n\n## 1. Cardinal rules [1][3]\n\n```xml\n<pluralRules locales=\"pt\">\n    <pluralRule count=\"one\">i = 0..1</pluralRule>\n    <pluralRule count=\"many\">e = 0 and i != 0 and i % 1000000 = 0 and v = 0 or e != 0..5</pluralRule>\n</pluralRules>\n<!-- pt-PT is listed separately with identical rules; pt-BR inherits pt -->\n```\n\nOperand meanings per UTS #35 [4]: `i` = integer part, `v` = visible fraction digit count, `e` = compact exponent (0 if none).\n\n- **one:** integer part is 0 or 1 → 0, 1, 0.5, 1.5, 1.99\n- **many:** (no fraction, nonzero multiple of 1,000,000) OR (compact exponent ≥ 6) → 1000000, 2000000, 1M, 2B, 1e6\n- **other:** everything else\n\n## 2. Number examples (pt-BR formatting: comma decimal, dot thousands) [1][3]\n\n| Number | Category | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 | one | i = 0 |\n| 1 | one | i = 1 |\n| 0,5 / 1,5 | one | i ≤ 1 |\n| 2 / 100 / 2,5 | other | |\n| 1.000.000 | many | multiple of 10⁶, v = 0 |\n| 1.000.001 | other | not a multiple of 10⁶ |\n| 1M (compact) | many | e = 6 |\n\nGotchas: **0 → \"one\"** and **1.5 → \"one\"** (Portuguese uses singular below 2: \"1,5 item\"). Use an explicit `=0` selector if you need special zero handling.\n\n## 3. Ordinal rules [2][3]\n\n```xml\n<pluralRules locales=\"pt\">\n    <pluralRule count=\"one\">n = 1</pluralRule>\n</pluralRules>\n```\n\n| Number | Category | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | one | 1º (primeiro) |\n| 2, 3, 11, 1.5 | other | 2º, 3º, 11º |\n\n(Gender suffix º/ª is not captured by plural categories.)\n\n## 4. ICU MessageFormat syntax for pt-BR [5]\n\n```\n{n, plural,\n  =0 {nenhum resultado}\n  one {# resultado}\n  many {# de resultados}\n  other {# resultados}}\n```\n\nOutputs: `1 → \"1 resultado\"` · `2 → \"2 resultados\"` · `1,5 → \"1,5 resultado\"` · `1000000 → \"1.000.000 de resultados\"` · `0 → \"nenhum resultado\"` (or \"0 resultado\" without `=0`)\n\n```java\n// Java (ICU4J)\nMessageFormat mf = new MessageFormat(\n    \"{n, plural, one {# item} many {# de itens} other {# itens}}\",\n    Locale.forLanguageTag(\"pt-BR\"));\nPluralRules rules = PluralRules.forLocale(ULocale.forLanguageTag(\"pt-BR\"));\nrules.select(0);         // \"one\"\nrules.select(1_000_000); // \"many\"\n```\n\n```js\n// JavaScript\nnew Intl.PluralRules('pt-BR').select(1);        // \"one\"\nnew Intl.PluralRules('pt-BR').select(1000000);  // \"many\"\nnew Intl.PluralRules('pt-BR', {type:'ordinal'}).select(1); // \"one\"\n```\n\nOrdinals in MessageFormat: `{pos, selectordinal, one {#º lugar} other {#º lugar}}` → 1 → \"1º lugar\", 2 → \"2º lugar\".\n\n## 5. Notes\n\n- `many` requires CLDR 42+ data (ICU 72+, modern browsers/Node); older runtimes silently never select it [7].\n- `#` renders the number locale-formatted (e.g., \"1,5\").\n- pt-PT differs from pt only in **pluralRanges** (range combination resolution, e.g. \"1–2 …\"), not in cardinal/ordinal categories [6].\n\n## Sources\n[1] CLDR `common/supplemental/plurals.xml` (cardinal rules) · [2] CLDR `common/supplemental/ordinals.xml` · [3] CLDR Language Plural Rules chart, unicode.org/cldr/charts/latest/supplemental/language_plural_rules.html · [4] UTS #35 LDML Part 3: Numbers, \"Language Plural Rules\" (tr35-numbers.html) · [5] ICU MessageFormat/PluralFormat docs, unicode.org/icu/userguide/format_parse/messages · [6] CLDR `common/supplemental/pluralRanges.xml` · [7] CLDR 42 release notes (many-category addition), cldr.unicode.org/index/downloads/cldr-42"